    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm working on a 12 week hypertrophy block for a client but they've completely stalled on their squat for the last 3 weeks. progress just stopped. can you explain how deloading or changing the volume/intensity might help them break through this plateau from a physiological standpoint?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A plateau in the squat, especially after a solid block, is a classic sign of overreaching, which is where a proper **deload** comes in. Physiologically, this period of stagnation is often due to accumulated fatigue, like a low-level, chronic strain on the central nervous system (CNS) and connective tissues.\n\nDeloading allows the body to recover from this cumulative stress, allowing the neuromuscular system to repair and adapt. If the fatigue is too high, simply pushing harder is counterproductive; a slight reduction in volume or intensity lets the body \"reset\" its readiness to handle heavy loads again. You might consider dropping the load by 40-50% while maintaining good technique for a week or two.\n\nAlternatively, if the CNS isn't the primary issue, a change in stimulus—like altering the volume or intensity—can force a new adaptation. For example, switching from high-rep hypertrophy work to lower-rep, higher-intensity strength work (or vice versa) can recruit different muscle fibers and challenge the system in a novel way, breaking the established pattern of adaptation. This is less about recovery and more about providing a new \"signal\" to the muscles.\n\nThink of it like this: the",
      "source": "target",
